---
onetaskgraph_template: 1
description: >-
  The design document a plan is read and approved as. The variables below are its data; how
  to write each answer is stated in this template's guidance comments, which no rendering
  carries.
variables:
  what:
    description: >-
      The What section's prose.
    type: text
    required: true
  why:
    description: >-
      The Why section's prose: why the work is worth doing, as its impact on the product,
      its users, development and resources, never an account of who asked for it.
    type: text
    required: true
  goals:
    description: >-
      The Goals section's entries, one string per goal, or `[]` for a plan whose tasks state
      none.
    type: list
    items: string
    required: false
    default: []
  architecture:
    description: >-
      The Architecture section's overview only, which may show a diagram as an image
      reference `![<alt text>](./<name>.png)` to an asset of this document. Each unit's
      subsection is rendered from `units`.
    type: text
    required: true
  units:
    description: >-
      One object per unit that changes, in reading order, with these keys: `name` (string,
      the unit's label, which a planned task's `unit` names); `repository` (string, the
      repository's short name, such as `onepipeline`); `part` (string, the package or path
      within that repository, or `""` when the unit is the whole repository); `summary`
      (text, one or two sentences, which may show a diagram as an image reference
      `![<alt text>](./<name>.png)` to an asset of this document); optionally `show` (a list
      of parts, the unit's canonical change seen from outside, rendered open after its
      summary); optionally `cost_reason` (string, the one reason every high-cost decision of
      the unit is high cost); `reversible` (a list of groups, the unit's trivial and
      low-cost decisions in reading order, or `[]` when it has none); and `decisions` (a list
      of objects, the unit's high-cost decisions, or `[]` when it has none). A group has
      `group` (string, its name in plain words, or `""` for one ungrouped run of items),
      `gist` (string, what its decisions amount to, in a few words; `""` when `group` is) and
      `items` (a nonempty list of objects, each with the string keys `title`, its one-line
      summary, and `text`, its one sentence). A decision has `name` (string, its heading),
      `justification` (string, who would have to act to undo it; omitted when the unit's
      `cost_reason` covers it), `gist` (string, one sentence), `show` (a list of parts, shown
      open: the canonical change, with at most one short concrete example beside it) and
      `details` (a list of at most three collapsed blocks, each with `summary`, a string
      stating the block's main idea, and `parts`, a list of parts; a block whose summary says
      all there is answers `[]` for `parts` and renders as a plain bullet, not a collapsed
      block). Each part is exactly either {type: "prose", text: string} or {type: "code",
      language: string, text: string}. Prose is nonblank Markdown without fenced-code
      blocks; inline code and an image reference `![<alt text>](./<name>.png)` to an asset of
      this document are allowed. Code is nonblank literal text, never a pre-fenced block.
      Language is a nonempty single-line fence info token: letters, digits, underscore, plus
      or hyphen; use `text` for untyped output. No extra keys and no unknown tags. A unit
      answered in an earlier shape is still accepted and renders exactly as it did: a flat
      `reversible` list of objects with the string keys `title` and `text`; decisions of
      `name`, `justification`, `content` (a nonempty ordered list of parts) and, only when it
      has examples, `examples` (a nonempty ordered list of parts); and legacy decisions of
      `name`, `justification`, `summary` and `artifact`, all strings. Decisions may use any
      of these exact shapes while deliberately regenerating a document.
    type: list
    items: object
    required: true
  acceptance_criteria:
    description: >-
      One criterion per entry, each a string.
    type: list
    items: string
    required: true
  graph:
    description: >-
      The plan's tasks and their dependencies exactly as `onetaskgraph project graph
      <project> --format json --group-by orchestrator.unit` prints them: the Planned tasks
      table lists every node, its unit (`group`) and the titles it depends on, from this.
    type: object
    required: false
    default: {}
  delivers:
    description: >-
      What each task delivers, as a short phrase on one line, keyed by the task's qualified
      id as `graph` names it.
    type: object
    required: false
    default: {}
  planned_tasks:
    description: >-
      Kept for documents written before `graph`: one object per task with five string keys,
      `task`, `unit`, `delivers`, `depends_on` and `location`. A new document answers `graph`
      and `delivers` instead and leaves this `[]`.
    type: list
    items: object
    required: false
    default: []
  dag_image:
    description: >-
      The bare file name of the image asset of this document that draws the plan's tasks
      and their dependencies, each inside the unit it changes, as its content references it
      with `./<name>`; `""` for a plan that shows no such image.
    type: text
    required: false
    default: ""
  plan_budgets:
    description: >-
      The plan's plan-level budget answers, its project's `orchestrator.plan-budgets`
      metadata as the plan store holds it, or `{}` for a plan with no project analysis: the
      shape the `plan-description` template's `plan_budgets` schema declares.
    type: object
    required: false
    default: {}
  budgets:
    description: >-
      Every budget of every task of the plan, read from the tasks' `orchestrator.budgets`
      metadata, or `[]`: each object is the budget as its task's `orchestrator.budgets`
      record holds it, the shape the `plan-task-budgets` template's schema declares, with
      four keys added, `node` (the id of the task
      that owns it), `location` (where the plan store reports that task), `anchor` (the
      anchor of the budget's heading in that task, or `""` for a legacy budget) and `link`
      (the destination of the budget's reference link: a local task's file relative to this
      document's directory, any other location as it stands, then `#` and the anchor).
    type: list
    items: object
    required: false
    default: []
  visual_changes:
    description: >-
      One object per visual change the plan expects, in the order the plan's visual spike's
      report lists them, with required non-empty string keys: `name` (a heading that says what
      changes), `description` (one or two sentences saying what the image cannot), `after`
      and optional `before` (each the bare file name of an image asset of this document, as
      its content references it with `./<name>`), and an optional boolean `featured` (shown
      open; at most three changes are featured, and a plan marking none features its first).
      Only absence of `before` selects a single image; null, empty and non-string values are
      invalid. `after` is always required. `[]` for a plan with no visual change.
    type: list
    items: object
    required: false
    default: []
  accepted_opaque:
    description: >-
      Copy verbatim from the project's `onepipeline.export_edges` metadata: ordered export edges,
      each with `source`, `to` and `accepted_opaque` (an ordered list of opaque pattern strings).
    type: list
    items: object
    required: false
    default: []
---
{#- A table cell is one line holding no bare column delimiter, whatever an answer says. -#}
{% macro cell(value) %}{{ value | replace("|", "\\|") | replace("\r\n", " ") | replace("\n", " ") | replace("\r", " ") }}{% endmacro %}
{#- Where the plan store reports a graph node: its `url` or `path`, or `""` for a node it reports none for. -#}
{% macro located(node) %}{% if node.location is defined and node.location %}{{ node.location.url if node.location.url is defined else node.location.path }}{% endif %}{% endmacro %}
{#- An expandable block's summary is HTML: `&`, `<` and `>` as entities, each backtick span as `<code>`. -#}
{% macro summary(title) %}{% for piece in title | replace("&", "&amp;") | replace("<", "&lt;") | replace(">", "&gt;") | split("`") %}{% if loop.index0 is odd and not loop.last %}<code>{{ piece }}</code>{% elif loop.index0 is odd %}`{{ piece }}{% else %}{{ piece }}{% endif %}{% endfor %}{% endmacro %}
{#- One prose or code part of a decision, a code part fenced longer than any backtick run it holds. -#}
{% macro part(one) %}
{% if one.type == "prose" %}
{{ one.text }}

{% else %}
{% set fence = namespace(value="```") %}
{% for unused in range((one.text | length) - (one.text | replace("`", "") | length)) %}
{% set fence.value = fence.value + "`" %}
{% endfor %}
{{ fence.value }}{{ one.language }}
{{ one.text }}
{{ fence.value }}

{% endif %}
{% endmacro %}
{% block document_guidance %}
{#
  THE READER. A technical product manager with no depth in this domain. They read this
  document instead of the plan, and must be able to accept a hard-to-reverse decision, argue
  with the architecture, or say the acceptance criteria do not describe what was asked.

  PROSE. Plain, simple, terse and precise, and no more technical than the plan's own goal
  statement. A term that cannot be avoided is defined once, in passing.

  SHOW, THEN TELL. What, Why and Goals are read closely and stay prose. Past them a reader
  scans for examples and for the literal shapes that change, so the rest of the document
  shows first: a diff, a record, a command and what it prints, an image. Telling is kept to
  one sentence wherever it is shown by default, and anything longer goes into a collapsed
  block whose summary already says its main idea.

  WHAT IS SHOWN IS THE PLAN'S OWN. Every example, shape and image comes from the plan's own
  contracts, tasks and spike reports: a diff of a shape a task states, a command and the
  output its contract names, a record the spike measured, a capture the visual spike took.
  None shows invented behaviour; where the plan states no concrete case, the document says
  the rule in one sentence rather than making a case up.

  LENGTH. A document is about as long as the design document this template replaces would
  be for the same plan. Exactness is spent on what is hard to undo, and everything else is
  said in a sentence. A high-cost decision shows about 600 characters of prose or less by
  default, its code excluded; the rest is in its collapsed details. The whole document's
  composed issue body, as `onetaskgraph document copy --dry-run` sizes it, is under 45,000
  characters, reached through terser writing and never by dropping what the approver needs. A plan that cannot
  be documented under 45,000 characters without losing needed content stays under GitHub's
  65,536-character refusal, and its writer reports the measured figure rather than
  truncating.

  SECTIONS. In this order: What, Why, Goals, Visual changes, Architecture, Budgets,
  Acceptance criteria and Planned tasks. Goals appears only for a plan whose tasks state
  goals, and Visual changes only for a plan with a visual change. A
  Budgets visibility follows `budgets_guidance` below. Planned tasks opens with an image of the plan's tasks and their dependencies only for a plan of
  more than one task with at least one dependency edge, and that image is generated by the
  flow from the plan, never drawn by the writer.
  There is no separate Contracts section, and no answer adds a heading of its own.

  IMAGES. A picture is an image asset of this document, never a Mermaid code block, which a
  Linear board does not render. Architecture is where a diagram is most often worth it, and
  the ARCHITECTURE guidance says when and how one is drawn, shown and stored; any other
  section may show one the same way where a picture explains better than prose.

  HIGHLIGHTING. No GitHub alert blocks (`> [!CAUTION]` and the like) anywhere. The bold
  "High cost to undo" summary, a reversible group's bold name and a visual change's heading are the
  whole of the highlighting.

  EXPANDABLE BLOCKS. What is only sometimes needed — a high-cost decision's details and why
  it is high cost, a group of reversible decisions, a visual change past the featured ones,
  a featured change's before image, the planned-tasks table — is collapsed, and the template
  alone writes each block, in the authored form onetaskgraph's README states under
  "Expandable blocks". A block's summary carries the main idea, so a reader who never opens
  it still has the short version. A summary's only markup is `<code>` and `<strong>`, never
  one inside the other, because those are the only tags the plan store translates to a
  Linear section's heading; the template writes both, so an answer writes backtick code and
  never a tag. No answer writes a `<details>` block of its own.

  NESTING. One block nests in another, and only one: the `Before` block inside a visual
  change that is not featured, holding its before image, written in the authored form
  onetaskgraph's README states for a nested block (its own `<details>` and `<summary>`
  lines and the blank lines around its body, on lines of the outer block's body, not
  indented). No other block opens inside another anywhere in the document.

  THIS TEMPLATE. Each section renders inside its own block: `what`, `why`, `goals`,
  `visual_changes`, `architecture`, `budgets`, `acceptance_criteria` and `planned_tasks`. Each section's guidance is a Jinja comment in a
  separate block, `<name>_guidance`, and this block, `document_guidance`, holds what applies
  to the whole document. Guidance is always a Jinja comment, never an HTML comment, so a
  rendered document carries none of it. The front matter states the data shape, what each
  answer and key holds; the judgment lives here. A repository overrides any one block, a
  section's guidance without its rendering or the other way round, through the engine's
  repository layer: `.onepipeline/templates/design-doc.md.j2` in its checkout, a file
  beginning `{% extends "onepipeline/host/design-doc.md.j2" %}` that redefines only the
  blocks it names.
#}
{% endblock %}
{% block what_guidance %}
{#
  WHAT. What is being built. What and Why are each a few plain sentences in plain terms a
  non-specialist reads, and carry no interface detail and no implementation detail.
#}
{% endblock %}
{% block what %}
## What

{{ what }}

{% endblock %}
{% block why_guidance %}
{#
  WHY. Why the work is worth doing, from the product owner's point of view: its impact on
  the product, its users, development and resources. It never narrates who asked for what:
  no "the user asked", "the user said" or "the brief says", and no quoted request. The
  record of what was requested and what was decided has one home, the plan's own
  description, and is not in this document; a task's own Why states impact too, never that
  record. Speaking of the product's users as the people the work affects is impact, not
  attribution.
#}
{% endblock %}
{% block why %}
## Why

{{ why }}

{% endblock %}
{% block goals_guidance %}
{#
  GOALS. The plan's tasks' Goals merged for the reader: one bullet per outcome the plan must
  reach, written from what the tasks' own `## Goals` sections state and inventing none. Where
  several tasks state one outcome, it appears once. A non-functional goal keeps the budget it
  names, by that budget's `id`, as its task states it. What a goal is is stated once, in the
  `goals` variable of this host's `plan-task` template. A plan whose tasks state no goals
  answers `[]` and this document has no Goals section.
#}
{% endblock %}
{% block goals %}
{% if goals %}
## Goals

{% for goal in goals %}
- {{ goal | replace("\n", "\n  ") }}
{% endfor %}

{% endif %}
{% endblock %}
{% block visual_changes_guidance %}
{#
  VISUAL CHANGES. The section shows; it does not describe. One entry per change the plan
  expects to make to what a user sees: a web page or component, a TUI, or a CLI's printed
  output. Its `name` is a heading that already says what changes ("`po slice` refuses an STL
  that is suspiciously small", not "Slice refusal"), and its `description` is one sentence,
  two at most, saying only what the image cannot.

  At most three changes are `featured`: the ones an approver most needs to see, shown open
  with the after image visible and the before image collapsed beneath it, because most
  readers already know it. Every other change is one collapsed block under its name holding
  its description and the after image, with the before image in a collapsed `Before` block
  nested inside it, so a reader opens only the ones they care about. A plan with three
  changes or fewer may feature all of them; a plan that marks none features its first, and
  past three the template features only the first three marked.

  The images are illustrative captures from a throwaway, mocked spike, not a preview of the
  finished work, which may differ where production concerns require. The `visual_changes`
  answer restates the plan's visual spike report, answer for answer, and each image it names
  is an asset of this document; the writer invents none. A plan with no visual change
  answers `[]` and this document has no Visual changes section.
#}
{% endblock %}
{% macro images(change) %}
![{{ change.name }}](./{{ change.after }})

{% if change.before is defined %}
<details>
<summary>Before</summary>

![Before: {{ change.name }}](./{{ change.before }})

</details>

{% endif %}
{% endmacro %}
{% block visual_changes %}
{% if visual_changes %}
{% set chosen = visual_changes | selectattr("featured", "defined") | selectattr("featured") | list %}
{% set chosen = chosen[:3] if chosen else visual_changes[:1] %}
## Visual changes

{% for change in chosen %}
### {{ change.name }}

{{ change.description }}

{{ images(change) -}}
{% endfor %}
{% set rest = visual_changes | reject("in", chosen) | list %}
{% if rest %}
**{{ rest | length }} more {% if rest | length == 1 %}change{% else %}changes{% endif %}:**

{% for change in rest %}
<details>
<summary>{{ summary(change.name) }}</summary>

{{ change.description }}

{{ images(change) -}}
</details>

{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
{% endblock %}
{% block architecture_guidance %}
{#
  ARCHITECTURE. Show the change; keep telling short. A reader scans this section for the
  shapes that change — a diff, a record, a command and what it prints — and opens prose only
  where they have an opinion. So everything shown by default is either a one-line statement
  or a literal artifact, and every paragraph of explanation sits in a collapsed block whose
  summary already says its main idea.

  The `architecture` answer is the overview: one or two sentences saying how the units fit,
  and the diagram when DIAGRAMS below calls for one. A unit is a repository, or a part of a
  project. Every unit that changes gets one subsection, headed
  `### <unit> — `<repository>` (`<package or path>`)`; the parenthesis appears only when the
  unit is part of a repository.

  Under each unit heading:
    - `summary`: one or two sentences saying what the unit is and what changes in it.
    - `show`, optionally: the unit's canonical change seen from outside, as `show` parts
      below — a command run and what it prints, a record before and after. A unit with no
      high-cost decision has nothing else to show, so it shows this whenever its change has
      a face a reader could look at.
    - `cost_reason`, when every high-cost decision of the unit is high cost for the same
      reason: that reason, once, in place of each decision's `justification`. A reason
      repeated word for word under every decision is one the reader learns to skip.
    - One `#### <decision>` per high-cost decision the unit owns, and only for those. Its
      `name` says what changes ("Copy status back without replacing authored content").
      Then:
        - `justification`, unless the unit's `cost_reason` covers it: who would have to act
          to undo it, in one short clause. It renders collapsed under "High cost to undo",
          as `cost_reason` does, because the heading already says the decision is costly.
        - `gist`: one sentence a reader could stop at.
        - `show`: the canonical change itself, open. A `diff` block for a changed shape, the
          full shape for a new one, a `--help` diff for a CLI, a text sketch for a UI, and,
          where the shape alone is hard to follow, one short concrete example beside it: a
          call and what it prints, a record before and after. Prose here is a one-line
          caption, never a paragraph. A behavioural decision with no artifact shows its one
          example — the situation and what now happens — rather than describing the rule.
        - `details`: everything else, as collapsed blocks — edge cases, exit statuses,
          compatibility, what was rejected and why, further examples. Each block's
          `summary` states its main idea as a plain-words claim a non-specialist follows
          ("With no filter, nothing changes"), never only a topic ("Exit codes"), so the
          collapsed line is itself the short version. A summary names no flag, field, file,
          command, code or number, and is about 70 characters at most; those exact names are
          what the block holds. A block whose summary says all there is answers `[]` for its
          `parts` and renders as a plain bullet. At most three blocks; a decision needing
          more is two decisions.
      The template owns every language label and safe backtick fence. Never author fences,
      and never disguise explanation as code.
    - `reversible`: the unit's trivial and low-cost decisions, grouped by what they touch.
      Each group has a `group` name in plain words with no code, rendered bold with its
      count, a `gist` saying in a few words what its decisions amount to, and `items`, each
      a `title` that says what the decision changes ("Adds a `--dry-run` flag to `copy`",
      not "Flag name", one line of about 70 characters, no final period) and a `text` of
      one sentence naming the thing exactly (the flag, key, field, route or command). A
      unit with four or fewer such decisions answers one group with an empty `group`, whose
      items render one block each; otherwise two to five groups, merging decisions that are
      one choice, each rendered as one collapsed line holding one bullet per decision. An
      item carries no level label, no artifact and no justification, because trivial and
      low cost read the same here.
    - Nothing else: no Kind, Owner or Status line, no Owes or May-assume bullets, and no
      links between sections.

  DIAGRAMS. A diagram earns its place when the architecture has three or more units, or a
  flow or a boundary between units, whose relations prose would need more than a short
  paragraph to convey. A single unit, or anything one sentence says, gets no diagram, and a
  document holds a few diagrams at most. Write it as a Mermaid source, render it with `just
  render-diagram <source.mmd> <name>.png`, show it from the `architecture` overview, a
  unit's `summary`, or a decision's `show` caption as `![<alt text saying what it
  shows>](./<name>.png)`, and give the file to the store with `--asset` when the document is
  stored.

  REVERSIBILITY. Three levels, defined by who would have to act to undo the decision:
    - trivial: only the unit that owns it, in one ordinary change.
    - low cost: parties we control, changing together. For example, several of our
      repositories or tools updated in step, a deprecation window, or a migration of data
      we own.
    - high cost: parties we don't control or can't list, or data we can't rewrite. For
      example, a public flag or field other people script against, a released artifact, or
      stored data that older installed versions or outside readers keep reading.
  When in doubt, the stricter level. These definitions are guidance a repository may
  override by replacing this block.
#}
{% endblock %}
{% block architecture %}
